   "text": "I read the structure in sections, left to right along the chain. The drawing is only partly legible, so the questionable points are listed first.\n\n**Uncertain readings**\n- **First glycerol-type unit:** I read its side arm as CH\u2013CH2\u2013CH2\u2013O (two carbons before the ether oxygen). The other two units have one carbon (CH\u2013CH2\u2013O). I followed the drawing as I saw it.\n- **Hexyl spacer:** I counted six carbons between the last phosphate and the amide NH.\n\n**Backbone**\n- The wavy bond at the top connects through a phosphate (P(=O)(OH)\u2013O).\n- Three glycerol-type units are joined by two propane-1,3-diyl phosphodiester linkers: O\u2013P\u2013O\u2013CH2CH2CH2\u2013O\u2013P\u2013O.\n- Each unit has a CH bearing the phosphate and a CH2\u2013O\u2013P arm. Its third arm is an ether to a propyl chain ending in NH\u2013C(=O)\u2013ethyl (propanamide).\n- The exception is the first unit, whose third arm is the DBCO-linked chain described next.\n- The last phosphate leads into a hexyl chain (CH2)6 ending in NH.\n\n**Left end**\n- The first unit's side arm is CH2CH2\u2013O\u2013(CH2)3\u2013NH\u2013C(=O)CH2CH2C(=O)\u2013N of a DBCO unit.\n- The DBCO unit is a dibenzoazocine fused to a triazole, and the triazole N carries an ethyl group.\n\n**Right end**\n- The hexyl NH is acylated by a succinyl group (C(=O)CH2CH2C(=O)) onto a second DBCO nitrogen.\n- That DBCO's triazole N carries \u2013CH2CH2\u2013O\u2013CH2CH2\u2013O\u2013CH2CH2\u2013O\u2013CH2CH2\u2013NH\u2013C(=O)\u2013CH2\u2013NH\u2013aryl.\n- The aryl group is the 4-position of an isoindolin-1-one whose N is attached to a glutarimide (2,6-dioxopiperidin-3-yl), a lenalidomide-type unit.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\n- The only stereo marking is the hashed bond from the glutarimide stereocentre to the isoindolinone N.\n- The priorities there are N > C(=O) > CH2 > H, and the hash puts N behind the plane. That gives (S), which is `[C@H]` in the SMILES as written.\n- The glycerol CH centres are drawn with plain bonds, so I assigned them no stereochemistry.\n\nSMILES: *OP(=O)(O)OCCCOP(=O)(O)OC(CCOCCCNC(=O)CCC(=O)N1c2ccccc2-c2nnn(CC)c2-c2ccccc2C1)COP(=O)(O)OCCCOP(=O)(O)OC(COCCCNC(=O)CC)COP(=O)(O)OCCCOP(=O)(O)OC(COCCCNC(=O)CC)COP(=O)(O)OCCCCCCNC(=O)CCC(=O)N1c2ccccc2-c2nnn(CCOCCOCCOCCNC(=O)CNc3cccc4c3CN([C@H]3CCC(=O)NC3=O)C4=O)c2-c2ccccc2C1"
